{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion","id":1501798,"title":"Multiverses in Wraith the oblivion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-01-31T04:16:17-08:00","id":1501798,"title":"Multiverses in Wraith the oblivion","content":"I am reading The book of Oblivion of Wr20, a book that a priori did not interested me so much, as it contains uber-optional information about the more high fantasy aspects of the setting (which I am less interested in) as for example, the Dark Kingdom of Swar, the Spectres or the Tempest as a plot device. It even depicts Jimmy Hendrix as a helldiver of an elite squad. But never, never I was expecting the references in the Labyrinth chapter to time travel and trans-universe-travel. It says that one particular character and, some time later, his relic airplane came from another &quot;continuum&quot;, which means another temporal continuum or universe, as it references things that havent happened. <br /> <br /> That perplexed me. I was expecting the Labyrinth as a place of horror, but instead I found there the highest fantasy peaks in all the setting. That could respond to the &quot;wyld&quot; being trapped there, as the Haunter guildbook says? I do not know. What do you think about it? This was for me constatation of Wraith being a game of high fantasy, and not so &quot;personal horror&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-31T20:56:51-08:00","id":1501846,"title":"","content":"I always thought of Wraith being high fantasy compared to the other Splats. A massive civilization of undead, fighting against an unheeding horde of Spectres that wish for full Oblivion to consume all, everyone having ghostly powers, the Tempest, Maelstroms, etc.<br /> <br /> I wasn't very interested in the 20th line of Wraith, mostly just focusing on the 2nd edition of it and its supplements, but I might just check Wr20 Oblivion. I'd also recommend <b>Doomslayers - Into the Labyrinth</b> for more stuff about the location.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-31T21:52:10-08:00","id":1501847,"title":"","content":"Ehhh...it's only as high fantasy as you want it to be in the same way how all of the other WoD gamelines are as high fantasy as you want them to be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4418-tasti-man-lh","name":"tasti man LH","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4418_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1850"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-01T00:09:34-08:00","id":1501852,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shakanaka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion?p=1501846#post1501846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always thought of Wraith being high fantasy compared to the other Splats. A massive civilization of undead, fighting against an unheeding horde of Spectres that wish for full Oblivion to consume all, everyone having ghostly powers, the Tempest, Maelstroms, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very true.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tasti man LH</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion?p=1501847#post1501847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ehhh...it's only as high fantasy as you want it to be in the same way how all of the other WoD gamelines are as high fantasy as you want them to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> Yes and no. I mean, obviously each storyteller can choose between the themes and atmosphere that proposes the setting. But the proposal is there, anyway, made by the authors. And Wraith, in contrast with the personal horror presumably is about, is rooted in the high fantasy, because the world itself is high fantasy (a completely new plane of a spectral ancient empire fighting against demon spectres, renegades and other empires). And the Labyrinth, in particular, seems to be the highest expression of those themes. It also happened with Vampire: The masquerade and the personal horror, which cannot be found anywhere in books as Time of thin blood or Dirty secrets of the black hand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24202,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","user":"Justycar","date":". 02-01-2023, 03:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-01T10:40:00-08:00","id":1501879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion#post1501798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But never, never I was expecting the references in the Labyrinth chapter to time travel and trans-universe-travel. It says that one particular character and, some time later, his relic airplane came from another &quot;continuum&quot;, which means another temporal continuum or universe, as it references things that havent happened.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understood that as a nod to the Adventure! game line and an option for STs to bring a pulp flavored feeling to Wr20. There's plenty of other references in BoO (heh!), just a bit earlier in the Labyrinth chapter there's an option to turn your game into Roadside Picnic / Stalker, even using the same terms for stalkers, artifacts and zones.<br /> <br /> Metaphysically, it kinda makes sense though. If there's a fixed point where all multiverses must converge, it's the Big Nothing at the end of it all. Some speculate the Neverborn to be remnants of failed, dead or rejected universes (ie Mage's <i>Qlippah</i>), which would fit into that idea.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-01T11:14:29-08:00","id":1501890,"title":"","content":"I suspected that reference to Adventure!, but I was not sure about it. I did not catch the one of Stalker though, I have not read the book. Metaphysically, I suppose it makes sense, if you conceptualize the World of darkness as a multiverse, the Oblivion could be a point of convergence, for sure. My surprise was more based on the narrative they gave to the Labyrinth. I do not know, I was expecting deep horror in the Labyrinth, some revelation about how to make better harrowings, hints about the malfeans, but not crossover with pulp and fantasy themes. Maybe, that was the problem, what I expect about Wraith in contrast with what the game actually seems to be about.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-01T11:50:46-08:00","id":1501894,"title":"","content":"Oh I agree that in terms of mood the Labyrinth was an odd choice to put these ideas. If I were to utilize them, I would probably relocate them to the Shadowlands- the Peril, Incorporated! pulp stuff would be better placed into a Renegade enclave and the Stalker aspects would be a nice touch to make the Shadowland wilds less mundane, for example. Maybe they wanted to make the Labyrinth a bit 'deeper' than just a pit of personal psychological horror, but imo it doesn't need that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-01T12:05:13-08:00","id":1501896,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion#post1501798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am reading The book of Oblivion of Wr20, a book that a priori did not interested me so much, as it contains uber-optional information about the more high fantasy aspects of the setting (which I am less interested in) as for example, the Dark Kingdom of Swar, the Spectres or the Tempest as a plot device. It even depicts Jimmy Hendrix as a helldiver of an elite squad. But never, never I was expecting the references in the Labyrinth chapter to time travel and trans-universe-travel. It says that one particular character and, some time later, his relic airplane came from another &quot;continuum&quot;, which means another temporal continuum or universe, as it references things that havent happened.<br /> <br /> That perplexed me. I was expecting the Labyrinth as a place of horror, but instead I found there the highest fantasy peaks in all the setting. That could respond to the &quot;wyld&quot; being trapped there, as the Haunter guildbook says? I do not know. What do you think about it? This was for me constatation of Wraith being a game of high fantasy, and not so &quot;personal horror&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Alex Corbin and his relic plane are a reference to the Trinity Continuum, which is an entirely separate multiverse from the World of Darkness (in my head canon I had him be the great-grandfather of Team Tonight leader Andre Corbin from Aberrant).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-02T05:20:58-08:00","id":1501943,"title":"","content":"The Neverborn are supposed to have crawled out of Oblivion back at the beginning and one of the possible explanations for this is that they are from other worlds and they find this one horrible and alien. So there is some precedent for the idea that Oblivion could be the gateway to other worlds for entities capable of surviving it. It's not an idea that fits well with the themes of Wraith except where eldritch monstrosities are concerned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-04T06:19:57-08:00","id":1502100,"title":"","content":"(Deleted post)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-06T10:28:23-08:00","id":1502313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion?p=1501943#post1501943\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Neverborn are supposed to have crawled out of Oblivion back at the beginning and one of the possible explanations for this is that they are from other worlds and they find this one horrible and alien. So there is some precedent for the idea that Oblivion could be the gateway to other worlds for entities capable of surviving it. It's not an idea that fits well with the themes of Wraith except where eldritch monstrosities are concerned.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Neverborn are clearly a Lovecraftian horror concept. I like them very much, and I like the eldritch unkown and unknowable monstrosities. I really like that WtO aint as popular as VtM, otherwise we would have books describing each one of their names, personalities, objectives and stories, much like it is for the Antediluvians, which is probably what I hate most about VtM.<br /> <br /> As for the Sci Fi Multiverse thing, not my cup of tea but hey, that's having options. Dont like it? Throw it away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-06T10:35:22-08:00","id":1502314,"title":"","content":"The only thing I truly dislike is the ritual to sever the Shadow away used by the Ferrymen. I think it runs completely opposite to everything the game stands for, not to mention that there really are no negative concequences for the Wraith (yes, the Shadow is set loose in the world, big deal, that's bad for the world and not for the individual, not to mention that even that is offset by the creation of a incorruptible Paragon).<br /> <br /> The concept is just simply bad all over, and I completely ignore it as if it doesn't exist.<br /> <br /> The Shadow is an integral part of every individual, and forcebly removing it should turn someone into a robot without free will or into a psychopath without any brakes. So I absolutely reject this idea","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-07T06:27:55-08:00","id":1502386,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kakost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion?p=1502314#post1502314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only thing I truly dislike is the ritual to sever the Shadow away used by the Ferrymen. I think it runs completely opposite to everything the game stands for, not to mention that there really are no negative concequences for the Wraith (yes, the Shadow is set loose in the world, big deal, that's bad for the world and not for the individual, not to mention that even that is offset by the creation of a incorruptible Paragon).<br /> <br /> The concept is just simply bad all over, and I completely ignore it as if it doesn't exist.<br /> <br /> The Shadow is an integral part of every individual, and forcebly removing it should turn someone into a robot without free will or into a psychopath without any brakes. So I absolutely reject this idea</div> </div> </div> </div>I haven't done much with Ferrymen except as cryptic and unreliable guides, so it really hasn't come up. I think it would only be an important element in a game where the players were Ferrymen. I wouldn't think losing the Shadow would impact a Wraith in the ways you describe, but we probably have different ideas in the role of the Shadow.<br /> <br /> Where I see a problem with Severance is it is hard to fit into the history of Wraith. It has to be Ferrymen exclusive or else why isn't it more widely known, so it has to tie into the Ferrymen origins or lore. Yet it also has to come late enough that the Shining Ones (who were originally Ferrymen) can fall to Spectredom. It functions almost exactly like what happened to Charon, but it can't be what happened to Charon. Etc. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-07T15:16:46-08:00","id":1502449,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion?p=1502386#post1502386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I haven't done much with Ferrymen except as cryptic and unreliable guides, so it really hasn't come up. I think it would only be an important element in a game where the players were Ferrymen. I wouldn't think losing the Shadow would impact a Wraith in the ways you describe, but we probably have different ideas in the role of the Shadow.<br /> <br /> Where I see a problem with Severance is it is hard to fit into the history of Wraith. It has to be Ferrymen exclusive or else why isn't it more widely known, so it has to tie into the Ferrymen origins or lore. Yet it also has to come late enough that the Shining Ones (who were originally Ferrymen) can fall to Spectredom. It functions almost exactly like what happened to Charon, but it can't be what happened to Charon. Etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes well, I dont use Ferrymen either (indee\u010f, they are mysteries and work better that way), but I dislike the concept of it, which is why I would've prefered they had never been given too much details about.<br /> <br /> The very idea of the Shadow is 100% based on Jungian psychology. And I really mean it. Jung analysis psychology in the form of the &quot;Archetypes&quot; (sounds familiar?), and one of if not the most important archetype is that of the... Surprise surprise... The Shadow.<br /> <br /> In Jungian psychology, the Shadow is... Guess what... The part of our personality that is responsible for all self criticism. It is that little voice in our consciences that say &quot;are you not gonna clean your room AGAIN? The entire week you have been postponing, and today one more day of it?&quot;.<br /> <br /> An imbalanced Shadow can create a neurotic and depressive individual. It's the person with a very low self image, that constantly belittle themselves. &quot;No wonder I dont have any friends, how would anyone want to be close to someone that is such a mess as myself&quot;.<br /> <br /> But the thing is, for Jung, the idea of the &quot;Self&quot; was not a monolithic and self contained thing, but rather a complex web that arises from the many Personas we have. For example, he identified many &quot;masks&quot; that we use for social life - you are one person when with your family, another at work and yet another with your girlfriend. Which one of those however is truly you, your true self?<br /> <br /> And you are yet a different person when alone in the confines of your mind. And besides those masks, there are the many archetypes, like Anima, the feminine side of males, and Animus, the masculine side of women, and several others, with special place for the Shadow.<br /> <br /> An unbalanced Shadow is problematic as I showed above, sure, but the Shadow is an integral and indispensable part of the Self. In order to have a balanced and health Self, we need to have ALL those aspects of our personality balanced and in harmony, and that includes the Shadow.<br /> <br /> A healthy Shadow helps an individual to correct mistakes and stay on proper course towards a healthy development. A healthy Shadow is &quot;constructive criticism&quot;. Instead of being the voice that crushes you, it's your inner voice that says &quot;are you just gonna quit like that, before giving 100% of what you have? And how will you be able to live with yourself if you give up right now?&quot;, and with that you push yourself to keep going and triumph, even when you're tired and willing to give up.<br /> <br /> That's why I said that a Wraith losing his Shadow would be nothing short than lobotomy. You'd either turn into an automaton, because you would lose that which is your driven force to act, or you would turn into a psychopath, because the Shadow is also that which gives you brakes on your impulses (God, I wish I could punch him in the face... But I am not an animal to do that, am I?).<br /> <br /> I love the aspect of the game that Transcendence is NOT the destruction of the Shadow, but rather INTEGRATION with it. It is the holistic view that Jung had about it too, that your Shadow archetype is NOT to be suppressed, but rather to be &quot;absorbed&quot; in a harmonic way within the Self.<br /> <br /> And this is why all the thing about cutting your Shadow off for the Ferrymen has always been an absurd and nonsensical idea, which I could only imagine happening on lobotomized people. I mean, try imagine yourself without your inner voice and inner criticism, without the ability to see what's wrong within yourself and absolutely no drive to change NOTHING. Imagine that your house is an absolutely a mess, with roaches and rats eating pieces of pizza in your carpet and you are absolutely fine with it and doesnt feel the slightest need to fix anything. That's more or less what lacking a Shadow would mean","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-08T06:54:23-08:00","id":1502512,"title":"","content":"I thought that might be where you were coming from. I agree that you can definitely see some fingerprints of Jungian psychology in Wraith. However, there are notable differences too.<br /> <br /> Most notably, a Wraith doesn't need the Shadow as any kind of driving force to pursue his Passions. It might occasionally provide a boost in the form of Shadow Dice, but it is not a dominant driver. Nor does it play any mechanical role in resolving Passions, except by accident. Nor does it play any role at all in developing new ones. You could view this as a flaw in the system mechanics, but I don't.<br /> <br /> The way I view it is that in wraiths the Passions (regular and dark) are terribly inflamed. That's why Wraiths even persist. The Psyche and Shadow are not healthy and functioning parts of a complete soul, they are the parts that desperately resist moving on.<br /> <br /> The Psyche is the part that cling desperately to the world. The Passions are not in any way balanced, they are drives the Psyche clings to with monomaniacal fervor even in the face of death. It doesn't need the Shadow to help push them. Indeed a healthy Shadow of the kind you describe would almost exclusively push towards resolving them.<br /> <br /> The Shadow, though, is not healthy, as it is the part that would rather cease than continue on in any form. Any self critical part of it bends towards self destruction. Any mediating role it has is subsumed by the Dark Passions, which fuel that desire for self destruction and also drive it to action.<br /> <br /> Perhaps we can say that if the excesses of the Shadow are cured it would function much like a Jungian shadow, but it is not functioning in that role for most Wraiths. The Psyche must drive and mediate itself, and it does.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-11T17:50:07-08:00","id":1502795,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion#post1501798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It says that one particular character and, some time later, his relic airplane came from another &quot;continuum&quot;, which means another temporal continuum or universe, as it references things that havent happened.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could it have been a reference to the movie <i>Biggles: An Adventure in Time</i>?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-12T09:39:16-08:00","id":1502845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1501798-multiverses-in-wraith-the-oblivion?p=1502512#post1502512\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought that might be where you were coming from. I agree that you can definitely see some fingerprints of Jungian psychology in Wraith. However, there are notable differences too.<br /> <br /> Most notably, a Wraith doesn't need the Shadow as any kind of driving force to pursue his Passions. It might occasionally provide a boost in the form of Shadow Dice, but it is not a dominant driver. Nor does it play any mechanical role in resolving Passions, except by accident. Nor does it play any role at all in developing new ones. You could view this as a flaw in the system mechanics, but I don't.<br /> <br /> The way I view it is that in wraiths the Passions (regular and dark) are terribly inflamed. That's why Wraiths even persist. The Psyche and Shadow are not healthy and functioning parts of a complete soul, they are the parts that desperately resist moving on.<br /> <br /> The Psyche is the part that cling desperately to the world. The Passions are not in any way balanced, they are drives the Psyche clings to with monomaniacal fervor even in the face of death. It doesn't need the Shadow to help push them. Indeed a healthy Shadow of the kind you describe would almost exclusively push towards resolving them.<br /> <br /> The Shadow, though, is not healthy, as it is the part that would rather cease than continue on in any form. Any self critical part of it bends towards self destruction. Any mediating role it has is subsumed by the Dark Passions, which fuel that desire for self destruction and also drive it to action.<br /> <br /> Perhaps we can say that if the excesses of the Shadow are cured it would function much like a Jungian shadow, but it is not functioning in that role for most Wraiths. The Psyche must drive and mediate itself, and it does.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, Wraiths are unhealthy psychological manifestations. I dont know if the writters designed it on purpose by using ideas on psychology, or if this has been a subconscious sublimation in the form of art, which is not uncommon - many Greek tales possess such attribute, like the myths of Narcise and Psyche.<br /> <br /> Wraiths have the two side of the same coin that drives us all, which Freud represented under the Greek Gods &quot;Eros&quot; and &quot;Thanatos&quot;, or the &quot;Pull towards life&quot; and the &quot;Pull towards death&quot;. Those two are our most basic psychic instincts - and in the game, they are clearly represented in both the Psyche (Eros) and the Shadow (Thanatos).<br /> <br /> An excess on either end can create an unhealthy mind - and that's where ALL Wraiths find themselves in.<br /> <br /> The &quot;Thanatos pulse&quot; (the Greek God of death) obviously leads to self destructive behavior, but an excess of &quot;Eros pulse&quot; (the Greek God of Love) leads to hedonism and nihilism.<br /> <br /> Ironically, suicidal tendencies are caused by EROS, not Thanatos. Suicidal people take their own life because, paradoxically, their pull of life is too big to bear. In their desperation to fulfill thei unquenchable thirsty to live, which apparently nothing can satiate, they take their own lives.<br /> <br /> For instance, the person that takes his own life after the end of a relationship or after discovering a cheating. &quot;If I can't live with that person, there's no purpose in living at all&quot;.<br /> <br /> I think that's well represented in the game in the form of the passions and fetters. Why do you get closer to Oblivion when you lose your passions and fetters? Because you are literally losing your &quot;will to live&quot;. It's the same mindset of suicidal though. &quot;What's the purpose of fighting against Oblivion if I can no longer protect my daughter?&quot;<br /> <br /> Every time a Wraith's Psyche can no longer indulge in his &quot;Eros pull&quot; (his passions), he gets closer to Oblivion - just like suicidals irl that see no way to achieve their life impulses (if I cant have my children/my dream job/the love of my life/my life's purpose - life is not worth it)<br /> <br /> Thanatos pulse on the other hand is self destructive in nature, but it is what keeps us in check. It's what allows us to deal with loss - otherwise our Life impulses would turn us all suicidal.<br /> <br /> But we all know what unbalanced self destructive actions cause - self sabotage. It is the case of people that are so afraid to be hurt by a loving partner that they actively sabotage the relantionship, so that you never become too dependant on that person to the point of being unable to live without him/her","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-12T10:18:57-08:00","id":1502850,"title":"","content":"Wraiths are therefore, by their very natures, VERY unhealthy people. Only a few of the dead actually turn into Wraiths, right?<br /> <br /> And the ones that do... Are those consumed by their <b>Passions </b>(Eros pulse).<br /> <br /> It's no wonder that many suicidals turn into the Silent Legion - aka become Wraiths, instead of immediatly plunge into Oblivion. If what a suicidal truly wanted was to end his existence, why would his passions bind him into the world, instead of his soul immediatly plunge into annihilation?<br /> <br /> Because suicidals dont want to embrace destruction, they want to LIVE. They want to live TOO MUCH, to the point that it becomes unbearable not being able to.<br /> <br /> All Wraiths refuse death, they refuse the end and refuse to let go or to move on, because their &quot;Eros pulse&quot;, their pulse of Life. Wraiths want TO LIVE.<br /> <br /> Even Spectres do. Because Spectres still have a Psyche and Passions. Thus, even thou Spectres represent individuals that surrendered to their worst self destructive impulses (Thanatos pulse), they still have the almost unbearable Eros pulses, the <b>desire</b> to live.<br /> <br /> Spectres that lose all of their Eros pulses... Disappear. Are consumed by Oblivion. Their self destructive impulses finally destroying every part of their personality (with the game's beautiful metaphor of Oblivion as a physical manifestation of that).<br /> <br /> Like I said however, the &quot;Thanatos pulse&quot; is necessary to accept the pain of loss. The death of a loved one, the end of a relationship, the changes in life.<br /> <br /> A Wraith's Shadow however is an unhealthy self destructive pulse.<br /> <br /> But in my opinion, this is were Transcendence gets in.<br /> <br /> Transcendence (mental harmony) is impossible without the Shadow, because in order to achieve it, you need to be able to integrate your self and your Shadow, in order to finally come to terms with your desires and your will to let go.<br /> <br /> When a Wraith suffers a loss and is throw into the Labyrinth to experience a delicious nightmare, this is an artistic representation of an individual's trauma.<br /> <br /> When you are in the middle of a traumatic experience, it feels like living in a nightmare for the time - exactly as being dragged by a nihil to the Labyrinth to be tormented by the Shadow.<br /> <br /> After that nightmare, we come out from the process of grief either broken or in acceptance of the loss. Healthy minds eventually come to terms to the loss, but if the individual cannot manage to overcome the trauma, he/she gets stuck into it. This is the entire mechanics of Harrowings.<br /> <br /> Without the Shadow to drag you to the Labyrinth however, how do you deal with trauma? When a Wraith who has no Shadow have one of their passions destroyed, how is the Wraith able to experience the trauma and heal from it?<br /> <br /> That's why Im saying, the very concept of removing one's Shadow dont make much sense without destroying the Wraith","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-13T06:05:33-08:00","id":1502923,"title":"","content":"There are two different issues here, Transcendence and Motivation.<br /> <br /> On a Transcendence level, Ferrymen are making it much more difficult for themselves to Transcend, though I think for a different reason than you mention. Harrowings are certainly the primary mechanism for bringing Wraiths to breaking points with their Passions and Fetters, and in the past may have even been a major way of resolving them. However, Wraiths are quite capable of resolving their Passions and Fetters without Harrowings. Indeed that was part of the purpose of the Far Shores, back before they fell.<br /> <br /> Where Severance really impacts Transcendence is in the resolution of the Dark Passions. The Ferryman is essentially giving up most of his ability to deal with those. He's deeply repressing them (externalizing them and burying them in the labyrinth) rather than processing them, and he will never have the same ability to address them again.<br /> <br /> But that's about whether the Ferryman ever Transcends, not about if he continues existing. On a motivational level Severance frees the Psyche to pull against Oblivion without resistance. Yes, in the inevitable spiral around the drain that is the Underworld, everyone who doesn't Transcend will eventually fall to Oblivion, but in this case eventually can take millennia and that's quite a bit longer than most Wraith's manage.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}